    "submission": "update-stage31.json",
    "summary": "No new work is claimed this turn: the run's time budget is essentially exhausted (a few minutes remain), every question in the record is locally answered or explicitly paused-with-discriminator, and there are no open necessary gaps, so I am not spending the remaining budget on a marginal item or an empty repeat. The path returned is the last accepted draft, unchanged, which the contract treats as no_change rather than as a new revision.\n\nState of the investigation as recorded, for the offline reviewer:\n\nVerified defects with retained receipts. (1) The leadership-transfer order: `TimeoutNow` carries no term at all and the receiver transitions without checking sender identity, term or membership, disabling both the pre-vote gate and the known-leader vote rejection — one order from a non-member claimed sender moved a stable follower to Leader at term 3 and deposed the serving leader, with a stale-term RequestVote control that changed nothing (receipt 8248101f2e7e47ecae3104f94cd0357b). (2) `GetConfiguration().Index()` returns a constant zero because the returned future is built from the published configuration only, and `nextConfiguration` enforces `prevIndex` only when it is non-zero — so a change guarded with the value the API returned was applied after an intervening change, while the same change guarded with the genuine index was rejected (receipt 11917033a9654d628e6d1452583ddd8e, with the predecessor deciding the same comparisons). (3) A node that recovered through InstallSnapshot keeps its log store index below the installed snapshot index, and `shouldSnapshot`'s unsigned delta stays permanently true, so the node writes a fresh full-state snapshot every SnapshotInterval while idle — 15 and 13 distinct snapshot IDs at the same index across two executions against 1 for the leader control (receipts dd59f37872d240548eff168eb821901f and c388285f090c4c1c85547bd6ddd9cd98).\n\nConditional finding: a below-protocol-2 leader encodes membership changes with the deprecated peer format, which cannot carry non-voters, and the constructor restores versioned configurations regardless of the node's protocol version — reachable in a rollback-style deployment, quorum-safe, no execution performed.\n\nNegative results worth keeping: the receiver's snapshot freshness check, the leader-lease-bounded completion of a stranded `VerifyLeader` (306ms against a 300ms lease with an error, control succeeding in 0ms), `ReloadConfig` rejecting an invalid merged configuration without changing anything, configuration propagation across 25 upgrade rounds (max 6ms, no node publishing old membership while holding the entry), pipelined replication's private send cursor, the heartbeat fast path's lock-protected mutation set, the log-store gap freedom and barrier contracts, existing-state detection, progress-accessor semantics, shutdown ordering, observation delivery being best-effort by default, and the Barrier enqueue-timeout distinction. Each carries its sources and its stated boundary; unresolved boundaries (for example the un-reproduced local failure of the captured upgrade test, and the fast path's inability to execute without a network namespace) are recorded rather than smoothed over."
